L151: Пиар\nL152: \nL153: Пиар\nL154: \nL155: Энергомаш\nL156: \nL157: Медицинские\nL158: технологии\nL159: \nL160: Фармацевтика\nL161: \nL162: Управление\nL163: персоналом\nL164: \nL165: Информационные\nL166: технологии\nL167: Информационные\nL168: технологии\nL169: \nL170: 【15†Image†static.tildacdn.com】\nL171: ## Как принять\nL172: участие\nL173: \nL174: 1 1\nL175: \nL176: Зарегистрируйся\nL177: на хакатон\nL178: \nL179: 2 2\nL180: \nL181: Собери свою\nL182: команду\nL183: \nL184: 3 3\nL185: \nL186: Выбери\nL187: кейс\nL188: \nL189: 4 4\nL190: \nL191: Создай лучшее\nL192: решение\nL193: \nL194: ## Как принять участие\nL195: \nL196: 1 Зарегистрируйся\nL197: на хакатон 2 Собери\nL198: свою команду 3 Выбери кейс 4 Создай лучшее\nL199: решение\nL200: \nL201: 【16†Image†thb.tildacdn.com】\nL202: \nL203: 【17†Image†thb.tildacdn.com】\nL204: \nL205: 【18†Image†thb.tildacdn.com】\nL206: \nL207: 【19†Image†thb.tildacdn.com】\nL208: \nL209: 【20†Image†thb.tildacdn.com】\nL210: \nL211: 【20†Image†thb.tildacdn.com】\nL212: ## КЕЙСЫ\nL213: \nL214: от топ-предприятий Москвы\nL215: \nL216: [Button: 3D/5D печать] [Button: ЛАССАРД] [Button: ОЧАКОВО] [Button: ПОЛИПЛАСТИК HR AI] [Button: LinQ]\nL217: ### Разработка интеллектуального сервиса для автоматического подбора параметров FDM 3D/5D-печати\nL218: Аддитивные технологии все активнее входят в промышленность, но настройка 3D-печати остается сложной задачей для новичков. Каждый слайсер содержит десятки параметров, от которых зависит качество, прочность и скорость печати.\nL219: Участникам предлагается разработать интеллектуальную надстройку над слайсером — MCP-сервер с чат-интерфейсом для автоматического подбора параметров FDM 3D/5D-печати по загруженной STL-модели, фото и текстовому запросу пользователя, поддерживающий дистанционное управление парком 3D/5D-принтеров и автоматически генерирующий оптимальные настройки и готовый G-код.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLessons learned from a 24-hour Hackathon (https://josy0ee-newsletter.beehiiv.com/p/lessons-learned-from-a-24-hour-hackathon)\nciteturn77view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://josy0ee-newsletter.beehiiv.com/p/lessons-learned-from-a-24-hour-hackathon\",\"lineno\":60}); Total lines: 130\nL19: ## 25th April\nL20: \nL21: ##### 12:00\nL22: \nL23: We had 2 ideas on the table, one was a voice assistant for GP reception, allowing expats to desribe their systoms in their language and automatically generating personalized, structured health documents for GPs; another is was a one-stop anti-discrimination platform: collect → structure → report. Beyond documentation, it could also guide users on how to respond or take actions if needed.\nL24: ##### 17:00\nL25: \nL26: We initally chose the 1st idea because it felt more like a “winning” project. But after mapping out the flow and right before we started building, we discovered similar products on the market, so we decided to pivot and focus on the anti-discrimination solution.\nL27: \nL28: Lesson 2: It’s not necessary to build something “brand new” at a hackathon.\nL29: \nL30: Lesson 3: Always define a timeline before you start. Without it, discussions can easily become endless.\nL31: ##### 18:30\nL32: \nL33: We finialized the user flow and aligned on the functionality and scope.\nL34: \nL35: ##### 20:30\nL36: \nL37: We stepped out for a fancy dinner and networked. Then we start to feel a bit anxious after hearing some teams say they were almost done.\nL38: ##### 23:00\nL39: \nL40: Time went incredibly fast once we began building, we split the task and locked in: Wei on UI, Eric on Development while I do storytelling. However, even with “vibe coding”, problem is still everywhere.\nL41: \nL42: Q1: What did the solution look like?\nL43: A: We envisioned it as a mobile app.\nL44: Q2: How do you build an app overnight?\nL45: A: Generating a webpage today is incredibly fast—you can spin one up in minutes with AI. But building a native app is a different story, especially when APIs and integrations get messy. On top of that, without an Apple Developer account, we wouldn’t even be able to publish it properly. So we had two practical options:\nL46: \nL47:   * A PWA (React-based web app)\nL48: \nL49:   * React Native with Expo\nL50: Given the time constraints, we chose a PWA. In hindsight, it was the right call—no one is going to download an app just to try a hackathon demo.\nL51: \nL52: 【4†Image†media.beehiiv.com】\nL53: \nL54: Claude analysis\nL55: \nL56: Lesson 4: A webpage is almost always more practical for a hackathon. If an app experience is necessary, go with a PWA.\nL57: ## 26th April\nL58: \nL59: ##### 00:30\nL60: \nL61: We left around 23:00. We briefly considered staying overnight, but I knew I’d completely crash if I pulled an all-nighter so we decided to go home in the end. Arriving home around 00:30, then I started building the mock demo without a functional product.\nL62: \nL63: 【5†Image†media.beehiiv.com】\nL64: ##### 03:30\nL65: \nL66: Wei and Eric sent over the app link, at the same time, I was trying to find a way to make the demo. That’s when I came across Remotion—a surprisingly powerful tool for animation creation. Instead of using traditional tools like AE, it generates videos through code (React) and a built-in player. It felt like a much faster and more flexible approach for what we needed.\nL67: \nL68: 【6†Remotion | Make videos programmatically www.remotion.dev†www.remotion.dev】\nL69: 08:30\nL70: Waking up, I saw that the UI had improved a lot through iteration. However, part of the functionalities weren’t working on my side, so Wei kindly recorded herself using the app for me.\nL71: \nL72: 【7†Image†media.beehiiv.com】\nL73: \nL74: UI iteration created by Wei\nL75: \nL76: There are generally two ways to approach a demo:\nL77: \nL78:   1. A full product promo video — usually with background music and eye-catching visuals, more like a marketing trailer.\nL79: \nL80:   2. A guided introduction — walking through the full app flow with human explanation.\nL81: We chose the second approach in the end. At the same time, I came across a few useful tools for demo recording—writing them here as a reminder for myself:\nL82: \nL83:   * 【8†Excalicord†www.excalicord.com】: Whiteboard+self-reocording — feels like a MacBook version of BeReal. Simple and free.\nL84: \nL85:   * 【9†Screen Studio†screen.studio】 — automatically tracks your cursor and zooms in/out during screen recording, creating a much more polished visual. However, exporting the final video requires payment.\nL86: Unfortunately, I couldn’t find a tool that records both the webpage and myself at the same time. So I ended up using the traditional setup: a Google Meet recording, and edit in CapCut.\nL87: \nL88: 10:00\nL89: I gathered all the necessary elements: the demo video, the Remotion animation, and my explanation recording.\nL90: Lesson 5: Even with the rise of AI-generated video tools, video editing is still a heavy and repetitive process. Whenever something is missing or misaligned (e.g. audio track order getting messed up), the whole workflow often needs to be redone.\nL91: ##### 11:30\nL92: \nL93: We finished the first version of the demo. After reviewing it together, we went through another round of edits.\nL94: ##### 12:00\nL95: \nL96: Ready to submit.\nL97: \nL98: This leads to the biggest takeaway from this hackathon:\nL99: \nL100: Lesson 6: Never cut it too close to the deadline—always start earlier.\nL101: \nL102: I thought I’d have more than enough time for final polishing before submission. In reality, everything took longer than expected, including exporting and uploading—even the final video was only one minute long.\nL103: In the end, I couldn’t paste our demo link into the submission sheet, so we ended up submitting it through GitHub alongside the codebase instead.\nL104: ## Outcome\nL105: \nL106: Unfortunately, we didn’t win. But seeing other teams’ projects was genuinely interesting. For the same theme, people approached completely different problems and framed the space in very different ways. It was also impressive to see how some teams managed to build surprisingly complete products in such a short time.\nL107: While we reached about 90% completion, others felt like they pushed things to 105%—polished, packaged, and fully presentation-ready. That gap was honestly impressive. But we knew we would continue working on it, because it’s something we genuinely feel is worth contributing to.\nL108: \nL109: It also reflects something about where things might be heading. With AI tools becoming more and more powerful, the real competition may shift from how well you build tools to how well you use them.\nL110: I spent around €2 of Claude Code credits, while I heard someone had already burned through €50 before dinner.\nL111: \nL112: After the event, I basically passed out as soon as I got home.\nL113: ### Keep reading\nL114: \nL115: 【0†View more】\nL116: \nL117: #### Jiaxin's Newsletter\nL118: \nL119: [Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input]\nL120: \nL121: [Input: Email]\nL122: \nL123: [Button: Subscribe]\nL124: \nL125: © 2026 Jiaxin's Newsletter.\nL126: \nL127: 【10†Powered by beehiiv†www.beehiiv.com】\nL128: \nL129: [Input][Input][Input]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nКак за 40 часов распознать всех поросят с помощью компьютерного зрения. Решение задачи на AgroCode Hack / Хабр (https://habr.com/ru/articles/653497/)\nciteturn77view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://habr.com/ru/articles/653497/\",\"lineno\":52}); Total lines: 164\nL36: Но нашли выход - 【15†репозиторий†github.com】, в котором решается похожая задача. Правда там оказалось всего 24 изображения в train и 6 в test. Мы сначала не поверили, что именно на этих данных обучалась модель, но решили попробовать тренировать свою, и она действительно заработала на наших данных. Но мы столкнулись с одним артефактом - сотрудники фермы, которые иногда заходили в станок, тоже распознавались как свинки!\nL37: 【16†Image†habrastorage.org】\nL38: \nL39: Для решения этой проблемы попробовали захардкодить слепую зону на входе в станок и отбрасывать все объекты, которые в нее попадают. К сожалению, это решение работало не на всех видео, потому что люди могли заходить и вглубь станка.\nL40: \nL41: 【17†Image: Красным цветом выделена слепая зона†habrastorage.org】Красным цветом выделена слепая зона\nL42: Тогда мы решили дообучить модель и использовать слепую зону для доразметки. Для обучения взяли только те видео, в которых нет человека или он не заходит дальше слепой зоны, а в качестве разметки использовали ответы первой обученной сети. Вторая сеть уже стала детектировать людей только если они находятся среди группы животных. Возможно бы и эта проблема решилась, если бы мы подольше пообучали сеть, но я случайно удалил код обучения в утро воскресенья (вот к чему приводит недостаток сна).\nL43: Далее перешли к трекингу. Для него просто использовали алгоритм 【18†DeepSort†github.com】, c которым мы уже работали ранее. Репозиторий, который применяли, также работает на tensorflow, и нам не пришлось добавлять дополнительные зависимости. К сожалению, этот алгоритм возвращает координаты свиней в другом порядке, поэтому пришлось модифицировать код, чтобы он принимал и возвращал еще и маски. Мы обнаружили, что после трекинга возвращаются растянутые маски.\nL44: Как решить эту проблему мы поняли уже после хакатона. Эта проблема возникла из-за того, что DeepSort использует фильтры Калмана и изменяет предсказанные координаты, и мы переписали код, чтобы трекер возвращал изначальный боксы.\nL45: Для решения последней задачи, оценки активности свинок, мы придумали простую эвристику на основе пройденного расстояния, которое рассчитывали с помощью трекинга.\nL46: \nL47: Подобрали несколько порогов и выделили несколько классов активности:\nL48: \nL49:   * серый: нулевая активность ( не двигается)\nL50: \nL51:   * зеленый: низкий уровень активности\nL52: \nL53:   * желтый: средний уровень\nL54: \nL55:   * красный: высокий уровень\nL56: \nL57: 【19†Image†habrastorage.org】\nL58: ### В итоге мы заняли 2 место :)\nL59: \nL60: 【20†Презентация работы  можете найти по ссылке†docs.google.com】\nL61: \nL62: 【21†А код решения посмотреть здесь †github.com】\nL63: \nL64: Теги:\nL65: \nL66:   * 【22†машинное+обучение】\nL67:   * 【23†компьютерное зрение】\nL68:   * 【24†нейросети】\nL69:   * 【25†хакатон】\nL70:   * 【26†видеоаналитика】\nL71: \nL72: Хабы:\nL73: \nL74:   * 【3†Машинное обучение】\nL75:   * 【4†Обработка изображений】\nL76:   * 【5†Работа с видео】\nL77:   * 【6†Хакатоны】\nL78: \nL79: +4\nL80: \nL81: 42\nL82: \nL83: 【27†2】\nL84: \nL85: -1\nL86: \nL87: Карма\nL88: \nL89: 【2†@NapoleonIT】\nL90: \nL91: Пользователь\nL92: \nL93: Подписаться\nL94: \nL95: Хабр доступен 24/7 благодаря поддержке друзей\nL96: 【28†Image: Хабр Карьера Курсы†habrastorage.org】\nL97: \nL98: Хабр Курсы для всех\nL99: \nL100: РЕКЛАМА\nL101: \nL102: Большая витрина: от крупнейших школ до частных авторов. Сравнивайте по цене, длительности, формату и выбирайте самый подходящий курс!\nL103: \nL104: 【29†Перейти†career.habr.com】\nL105: \nL106: 【27†Комментарии 2】\nL107: \nL108: 【30†ЕЖЕДНЕВНЫЙ ХАБР | 23 ОКТ 2026】\nL109: \nL110: 16K\nL111: ## 【31†Представлен открытый нео-гротескный шрифт Nordwand-Mono, разработанный для программирования】\nL112: \nL113: Разработчик Танги Вивье 【32†опубликовал†github.com】 проект открытого компактного нео-гротескного шрифта под названием 【33†Nordwand-Mono†github.com】. Решение специально разработано с нуля на Python для программирования в терминале и в целом для работы с Neovim.\nL114: \nL115: 【34†Image: Представлен открытый нео-гротескный шрифт Nordwand-Mono, разработанный для программирования†habrastorage.org】\nL116: ### 【35†Сопли — это биоинженерный шедевр】\nL117: \nL118: ### 【36†Настоящую крипту запретили в РФ с 2027: полный юридический разбор нового закона】\nL119: \nL120: ### 【37†8 новых терминов в области ИИ, которые полезно знать】\nL121: \nL122: 2【30†Ещё】\nL123: ## Публикации\nL124: \nL125: [Button: Лучшие за сутки][Button: Похожие]\nL126: \nL127: Показать лучшие за всё время\nL128: \nL129:   *   *   *   *   * Ваш аккаунт\nL130: \nL131:   * 【1†Войти】\nL132:   * 【38†Регистрация】\nL133: \nL134: Разделы\nL135: \nL136:   * 【0†Статьи】\nL137:   * 【39†Новости】\nL138:   * 【40†Хабы】\nL139:   * 【41†Компании】\nL140:   * 【42†Авторы】\nL141:   * 【43†Песочница】\nL142: \nL143: Информация\nL144: \nL145:   * 【44†Устройство сайта】\nL146:   * 【45†Для авторов】\nL147:   * 【46†Для компаний】\nL148:   * 【47†Документы】\nL149:   * 【48†Соглашение†account.habr.com】\nL150:   * 【49†Конфиденциальность†account.habr.com】\nL151: \nL152: Услуги\nL153:   * 【50†Корпоративный блог†company.habr.com】\nL154:   * 【51†Медийная реклама†company.habr.com】\nL155:   * 【52†Нативные проекты†company.habr.com】\nL156:   * 【53†Образовательные программы†company.habr.com】\nL157:   * 【54†Стартапам†company.habr.com】\nL158: \nL159: Настройка языка【55†Техническая поддержка】На информационном ресурсе применяются 【56†рекомендательные технологии】\nL160: \nL161: © 2006–2026, 【57†Habr†company.habr.com】\nL162: \nL163: 【58†Image】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\"Start Each feature with an Acceptance Test\" => end-to-end ? (https://groups.google.com/g/growing-object-oriented-software/c/uKtktAVhWLM)\nciteturn77view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/growing-object-oriented-software/c/uKtktAVhWLM\",\"lineno\":770}); Total lines: 1107\nL744: > On 17 June 2013 13:36, Nat Pryce <nat....@gmail.com> wrote:\nL745: >\nL746: >> Brittleness: ene-to-end tests use all of the 'moving parts' of your application (including infrastructure). System tests ensure that all these components play together nicely. Acceptance tests demonstrate that specific application functionality has been delivered. Why expose your acceptance tests to the risk of intermittent/transient failures? Or to put the other way - what's the benefit of doing this?\nL747: Speed is a concern, I agree.  But brittleness/intermittency is highlighting design issues.\nL748: \nL749: Why is the system brittle? Why does it intermittently fail? Or why do the tests intermittently fail to understand what the system is doing?  Those are issues I'd want to address.  I don't want a system that has intermittently failing behaviour when deployed, or for which management operations intermittently fail.\nL750: What's the benefit of doing this?  It gives you more options as to implementation strategy and drives out more extensive robust monitoring and management interfaces.  If acceptance tests only run against the domain model, the only way to add functionality is to add code to the domain model.  Deleting the domain model entirely won't be easy to do, or even envisage.  Is that worth the slowness and brittleness?  It depends on the system.\nL751: I too think that the best approach, as you wrote in your other post, is to be able to target tests at different amounts of the system.  But I've always found that difficult: how to stop implicit assumptions about the level at which you're testing make it hard to retarget them at other levels of the system?\nL752: \nL753: --Nat\nL754: \nL755: 【6†Image: Michael Azerhad's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com】\nL756: ### Michael Azerhad\nL757: \nL758: unread,\nL759: \nL760: Jun 17, 2013, 5:55:13 PM 6/17/13\nL761: \nL762: \nL763: \nL764: \nL765: \nL766: \nL767: \nL768: to growing-object-o...@googlegroups.com\nL769: \nL770: Seb Rose, do you have a brief sample somewhere showing the concept of that cucumber tag interchanging the scope (end-to-end or domain) of the test ?\nL771: \nL772: How could a scenario implementation oriented UI first, like Selenium be....mocked and tackling domain model (service rather), without altering totally the implementation ?\nL773: \nL774: By the way, nice article on your blog :)\nL775: \nL776: Michael\nL777: \nL778: \nL779: 【9†Image: Nat Pryce's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com】\nL780: ### Nat Pryce\nL781: \nL782: unread,\nL783: \nL784: Jun 18, 2013, 8:45:59 AM 6/18/13\nL785: \nL786: \nL787: \nL788: \nL789: \nL790: \nL791: \nL792: to growing-object-o...@googlegroups.com\nL793: \nL794: On 17 June 2013 01:23, Michael Azerhad <michael...@gmail.com> wrote:\nL795: > ... I chose to start to implement the feature by creating a unit test of my \"future\" controller (I use Play framework). So, UserControllerTest is firstly created, leading to create a UserServicesImplTest.\nL796: >\nL797: > Implementation of the UserServicesImplTest is totally trivial, mocking a UserRepository returning an empty list. Of course, I create also a real UserRepository dealing with a simple NoSQL request to find Users. This latter will be call by the end-to-end acceptance test.\nL798: >\nL799: > I make UserServiceImplTest passes easily and then UserControllerTest (containing a mocked UserServicesImplTest in order to be truly isolated)\nL800: >\nL801: > (I agree that a real TDD procedure doesn't know anything about layers at the very beginning... but in order to speed up a little, I allow me just this step jump.)\nL802: No! :-)\nL803: \nL804: You're missing the fundamental aspect TDD if you start by thinking up a load of layers and patterns, write tests that assert that code fits that design and then write code to fit into the design that's now concreted in by your tests.\nL805: Really try to start with the *smallest* test that you can.  Try to write the *simplest* implementation that makes the test pass.  Let structure emerge by refactoring.  As structure emerges choose meaningful names that expresses how the code relates what the system's users do and how they talk about the work. You may be surprised as to the design that emerges.  E.g. maybe there will be no classes named ThingyImpl, WotsitService or GizmoRepository.\nL806: For a start, how do your users or customer(s) refer to users of your system?  As a user base?  A user population?  Cohort? Community?  I bet they don't say \"User Repository\"!\nL807: The point of a walking skeleton is to help you understand the *external* integration points between your system (and team) and the other system's (and teams) you need to integrate with.  That may be third-party services, operations teams, network monitoring infrastructure mandated within the organisation, etc. etc. I would not include a database component that's fully under your control and that you deploy as part of your app in a walking skeleton.\nL808:  That's just an internal implementation choice that one can very often defer until later.\nL809: --Nat\nL810: \nL811: --\nL812: 【10†http://www.natpryce.com†www.natpryce.com】\nL813: \nL814: 【6†Image: Michael Azerhad's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com】\nL815: ### Michael Azerhad\nL816: \nL817: unread,\nL818: \nL819: Jun 18, 2013, 9:06:39 AM 6/18/13\nL820: \nL821: \nL822: \nL823: \nL824: \nL825: \nL826: \nL827: to growing-object-o...@googlegroups.com\nL828: \nL829: Understood for layers :) I will follow a pure TDD procedure so, letting my code to emerge them, if happens, without anticipating them :)\nL830: \nL831: And I agree, customers may not even know what is a database.. :)\nL832: Thus, why do we qualify the walking skeleton to be \"end-to-end\"? Should I rather deal with in-memory storage (simple POJO for instance) in order to be more flexible to future decision (deferring storage choices) if my TDD makes emerge the need for a storage?\nL833: \nL834: Why the choice of UI implementation, like GOOS presents with a Swing interface, is made at walking skeleton time? Why this choice should not be deferred, like database should?\nL835: \nL836: Can't I say: \"Customers may not even know about kind of UI interfaces\"?\nL837: By \"end-to-end\" terms, I understand the notion of concrete mechanisms…not really just a simple \"understanding\" of necessary components…\nL838: \nL839: I'm a bit confused so :)\nL840: \nL841: Thanks Nat :)\nL842: \nL843: \nL844: \nL845: 【23†Image: Ville Svärd's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com】\nL846: ### Ville Svärd\nL847: \nL848: unread,\nL849: \nL850: Jun 18, 2013, 9:09:39 AM 6/18/13\nL851: \nL852: \nL853: \nL854: \nL855: \nL856: \nL857: \nL858: to growing-object-o...@googlegroups.com\nL859: \n"
